CORE
main.c
--unwind 3 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
Each thread picks a region by a symbolic index and protects that region's
datum with that region's own mutex -- the 09-regions / 06-symbeq family.
Two threads with different indices touch different data; two threads with
the same index take the same lock. No case races.

A read of `data[i]` at a symbolic `i` on a *static* array (not behind a
pointer, so #305's fix does not touch it) was recorded by
`goto_symex_statet` as a read of every element instead of the one element
actually read, which can pair it against an unrelated write and report a
race that never occurs.

Before the fix this program was reported FAILED; it is race-free and must be
SUCCESSFUL. No corpus benchmark currently exercises this path -- this test is
the only coverage for it. See `static_array_symbolic_index_regions_nolock`
for the genuine-race counterpart.
